Q: What&#39;s the difference between an image&#39;s CL_MEM_ASSOCIATED_MEMOBJECT and CL_IMAGE_BUFFER? A: <p>Both <code>CL_IMAGE_BUFFER</code> and <code>CL_MEM_ASSOCIATED_MEMOBJECT</code> return the same associated buffer for an image.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>cl_mem buf1, buf2;
clGetImageInfo(image, CL_IMAGE_BUFFER, sizeof(buf1), &amp;buf1, NULL);
clGetMemObjectInfo(image, CL_MEM_ASSOCIATED_MEMOBJECT, sizeof(buf2), &amp;buf2, NULL);
// buf1 == buf2 (or both NULL if the image was not created from a buffer)
</code></pre>
<p><code>CL_IMAGE_BUFFER</code> arrived in OpenCL 1.2 but was deprecated in 2.0. Since <code>CL_MEM_ASSOCIATED_MEMOBJECT</code> has been around since 1.1 and handles both images and sub-buffers, I'd just use that. <a href="https://github.com/KhronosGroup/OpenCL-Docs/blob/main/api/appendix_e.asciidoc" rel="nofollow noreferrer">The deprecation note in OpenCL 3.1.1</a> (published recently - April 2026) confirms it's the intended replacement.</p>
